{
  "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
  "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Dexter Earl Kemp)",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Rule 60(b)(6) governs this case because Rule 60(b)(1) does not. That is so for two independent reasons. First, Rule 60(b)(1) does not cover legal errors. Second, it does not cover judicial errors."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Dexter Earl Kemp)",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If (b)(1) covered legal errors as well, that would contravene the structure of the rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Adler, are you conceding that your -- that the plain meaning of the word \"mistake\" doesn't work for you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Dexter Earl Kemp)",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The only other rules that we know, putting aside (b)(1), that cover legal errors are (b)(4) for void judgments and (b)(5). Those are pretty narrow categories, and they're also mutually exclusive with (b)(6)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What about the 60(b)(1) that the courts have imposed a deadline saying reasonable time means within 30 days or 60 days or what have you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Dexter Earl Kemp)",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the only way to write it is based on the government's definition of unintentional and obvious is the -- is what mistake means."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: You can proceed with your argument."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: But the statute -- not the statute, sorry --the rule doesn't -- nothing on the face of the rule excludes courts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Snyder",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "indiscriminately to all errors. Other times, the word is used in a narrower sense that covers only inadvertent errors. But, under either of those definitions, the district court's error here clearly qualifies as a mistake that the court could have addressed through a timely Rule 60(b)(1) motion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Snyder, you argue in your brief, I think, that not every error is a mistake. I don't know what the difference is."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Why would we do that? It just seems like asking for a whole lot of litigation about the difference between an obvious mistake -- suppose you interpret the statute one way and then you read some more in -- in response to the 60(b) motion and you say, you know, I think I got it wrong. Does that qualify as a mistake or not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Kavanaugh, you were asking about what's wrong with the Second Circuit's approach of sort of imposing this appeal deadline."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-21-5726",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "bc459edd86d7480f8d2edab64bbc2c9a6b432cb549b031566588ea9cddf12d7a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-bc459edd86d7-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
